QB.add(7,[{"id":7001,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"Which set correctly lists the three general principles of development (Woolfolk et al. 2013) used?","o":["Different rates; relatively orderly; gradual","Same rate; relatively orderly; gradual","Different rates; random order; gradual","Different rates; relatively orderly; in sudden stages","Same rate; random order; in sudden stages"],"e":"The three principles are: <b>people develop at different rates</b>, <b>development is relatively orderly</b> (simple before complex, in roughly the same order), and <b>development takes place gradually</b> (building step by step – “we don't get taught quantum mechanics” first). They apply to language in particular, which is why a doctor should not assume a child's language level from age alone.","w":["People develop at different rates.","Development is relatively orderly (simple before complex).","Development takes place gradually.","All three are wrong."],"k":"Different rates, orderly, gradual.","rn":[["","What are the 3 general principles of development?","What are the 3 general principles of development?","People develop at different rates; Development is relatively orderly; Development takes place gradually"]]},{"id":7002,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"The nephew took ages to start speaking, then picked it up very quickly, reaching the same end point as his niece. Which principle does this illustrate, and what is the implication for doctors?","o":["People develop at different rates, so check the child’s level","Development is orderly, so all children talk at the same age","Development is gradual, so late talkers rarely catch up","Development is orderly, so age reliably predicts language level","People develop at different rates, so milestones are meaningless"],"e":"The nephew/niece example shows that <b>people develop at different rates</b> but reach the same end point. The practical point: use age as a <b>starting guess</b>, then <b>check</b> the child's level (ask questions, monitor responses, ask them to repeat back).","w":["Rates differ.","He caught up quickly.","Age is only a starting guess.","Milestones are still a useful guide."],"k":"Different rates → gauge each child.","rn":[["","Why not judge a child's language level from age alone?","Why not judge a child's language level from age alone?","Different rates of development; Age is a first guess; Then check"]]},{"id":7003,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","f":"la_chart","s":"A child recognises their own name, says a few words and can follow simple instructions such as “wave bye-bye”, but does not yet combine words. What is the most likely age?","o":["About 1 year","About 18 months","About 2 years","About 3 years","About 6 months"],"e":"<b>By 1</b>: a <b>few words</b>, recognises own <b>name</b> (turns and listens), follows <b>simple instructions</b>. First words are simple and concrete (“mama”, “dada”). Two-word sentences come by 2.","w":["Not a lecture milestone; these are age-1 abilities.","Two-word sentences are expected by 2.","By 3: ~450 words and short sentences.","Not a lecture milestone."],"k":"Age 1: few words, name, simple instructions.","rn":[["","What can a child do with language by age 1?","What can a child do with language by age 1?","Recognises own name; Says a few words; Follows simple instructions"]]},{"id":7004,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"A child says “Michael drink” to get a drink. Which milestone does this show, and at what age is it typical?","o":["Two-word sentences making desires known, by 2","Two-word sentences making desires known, by 1","Short sentences and some plurals, by 3","Telling simple stories, by 4","Following simple instructions, by 1"],"e":"<b>By 2</b>: about <b>20 words</b>, <b>two-word sentences</b> including names, and <b>making desires known</b> – combining words to get things to happen. Clinically, a 2-year-old can make it known they are in pain and want it to stop.","w":["By 1: a few words only.","That is age 3.","That is age 4.","That is a different milestone."],"k":"Age 2: two-word sentences, making desires known.","rn":[["","What can a child do with language by age 2?","What can a child do with language by age 2?","Two-word sentences, including names; Makes desires known"]]},{"id":7005,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"Approximately how many words does a child use at each age from 2 to 5?","o":["~20 (2), ~450 (3), ~1,000 (4), ~1,500 (5)","~20 (2), ~100 (3), ~450 (4), ~1,000 (5)","~200 (2), ~450 (3), ~1,500 (4), ~3,000 (5)","~50 (2), ~450 (3), ~1,000 (4), ~2,000 (5)","~20 (2), ~450 (3), ~1,500 (4), ~2,000 (5)"],"e":"Vocabulary: a few words (1) → <b>~20</b> (2) → <b>~450</b> (3) → <b>~1,000</b> (4) → <b>~1,500</b> (5) (Woolfolk et al., 2013). The lecturer's trick: digits in the vocabulary size – 1 = single digits, 2 = tens, 3 = hundreds, 4 = thousands.","w":["Too low from 3 onward.","Too high at 2.","Not the figures.","4 is ~1,000; 5 is ~1,500."],"k":"Vocabulary: few → 20 → 450 → 1,000 → 1,500."},{"id":7006,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","f":"la_chart","s":"Between which ages is the biggest relative jump in vocabulary – the “explosion of words”?","o":["Between 2 and 3","Between 1 and 2","Between 3 and 4","Between 4 and 5","After age 5"],"e":"The lecturer described a real <b>explosion of words</b> at age 3, from <b>tens into hundreds</b> (~20 at 2 → ~450 at 3).","w":["A few words to ~20.","~450 to ~1,000.","~1,000 to ~1,500.","Not covered."],"k":"Word explosion: age 2→3.","rn":[["","Between which ages is the \"explosion of words\"?","Between which ages is the \"explosion of words\"?","2 to 3; Tens to hundreds of words"]]},{"id":7007,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"A 3-year-old says “My foots hurt.” What does this error show about their development?","o":["They have extracted the plural rule and over-applied it","They are imitating an adult who says “foots”","They have learned “foots” as a single new word from listening to adults","They have not yet learned any plural forms","They are being reinforced for this error"],"e":"At 3 children form some <b>plurals</b>; “my foots hurt” shows them <b>experimenting with grammar</b>: spotting the rule “add -s” and <b>over-applying</b> it. No adult says “foots”, so it cannot be imitation – which is also evidence against pure behaviourism.","w":["No adult says “foots”.","No adult says “foots”; it shows a rule.","They have learned the -s rule.","Not the point."],"k":"“My foots” = over-applied rule, not imitation.","rn":[["","What does \"My foots hurt\" show?","What does \"My foots hurt\" show?","Extracted the plural -s rule; Over-applied it; Not imitation"]]},{"id":7008,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"A doctor wants to locate a 3-year-old's pain. Which approach best matches the child's expected abilities?","o":["Ask “where does it hurt?” and ask them to point","Ask them to describe the pain in abstract terms","Ask them to say when it started, using past tense","Ask several short questions together to save time","Ask only the parent, as 3-year-olds cannot answer"],"e":"By 3, children identify <b>body parts</b> and answer <b>“where?”</b> questions, so you can ask “where does it hurt?” and they can <b>point</b> (e.g. to their knee). Abstract terms and multiple questions are not appropriate.","w":["Keep it literal and concrete.","Past tense is age 5.","One question at a time.","3-year-olds answer “where?” questions."],"k":"Age 3: “where?” questions and pointing.","rn":[["","At what age can a child point to where it hurts?","At what age can a child point to where it hurts?","By 3"]]},{"id":7009,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"Which set of abilities is typical by age 4?","o":["Tells stories, 5-word sentences, knows last name and street","Uses past tense, asks questions, identifies colours and shapes","Short sentences, identifies body parts, answers “where?”","Two-word sentences and makes desires known","Tells stories, 5-word sentences, uses past tense"],"e":"<b>By 4</b>: ~1,000 words; <b>tells stories</b>; <b>5-word sentences</b>; knows <b>last name, street name and nursery rhymes</b>. In the video the niece made mistakes in her rhyme and nobody corrected her.","w":["Age 5.","Age 3.","Age 2.","Past tense is age 5."],"k":"Age 4: stories, 5-word sentences, rhymes.","rn":[["","What does a 4-year-old know?","What does a 4-year-old know?","Last name; Street name; Nursery rhymes"],["","What can a child do with language by age 4?","What can a child do with language by age 4?","Tells stories; Uses 5-word sentences"]]},{"id":7010,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"About how many words does a typical 5-year-old use?","e":"By 5 (~1,500 words): past tense, asks questions, identifies colours and shapes.","k":"~1,500.","a":["1500","1,500","about 1500","around 1500"]},{"id":7011,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"Why are a 1-year-old's first words typically “mama” or “dada” rather than words like “regret”?","o":["First words are simple, concrete things nearby","First words are words that parents reward most","The LAD favours words with repeated syllables","Abstract words are heard less often by infants","First words are copied from other children"],"e":"First words are <b>simple, concrete</b> things in the child's <b>immediate environment</b>, never abstract ideas. This links to child-directed speech being <b>literal and concrete</b> and focusing on the here and now.","w":["The point is concreteness.","Not stated.","The point is concreteness.","Not stated."],"k":"First words: concrete, immediate environment.","rn":[["","What are a 1-year-old's first words like?","What are a 1-year-old's first words like?","Simple, concrete things; Nearby; e.g. \"mama\", \"dada\""]]},{"id":7012,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"What term means the rules for combining words, e.g. plural endings?","e":"By 5 children have learned to distinguish and pronounce words, understand meanings, grammar (rules for combining words) and pragmatics.","k":"Grammar.","a":["grammar","syntax","morphology"],"rn":[["","What 3 language levels have children learned by age 5?","What 3 language levels have children learned by age 5?","Meaning (semantics); Grammar; Pragmatics"]]},{"id":7013,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"A child asks “Can we play?” and Dad says “Daddy’s tired.” The child understands “not now”. Name this language skill.","e":"Pragmatics: how we do things with words – what people mean beyond the literal words.","k":"Pragmatics.","a":["pragmatics","pragmatic"],"rn":[["","Give an example of pragmatics in a child's language.","Give an example of pragmatics in a child's language.","\"Can we play?\"; \"Daddy's tired.\"; Child understands: no, not now"]]},{"id":7014,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"Why is distinguishing words described as an impressive achievement?","o":["Speech is a continuous string of noises without pauses","Adults pause clearly between words for children","Children are taught word boundaries explicitly","Children hear mainly single words, not sentences","Word boundaries are learned only after starting school"],"e":"When people speak they don't pause between words; it is “a string of noises”, yet 2–3-year-olds work out where one word ends and the next begins. Some children learn more than one language before age 5.","w":["No pauses.","Not taught.","They hear continuous speech.","By 2–3."],"k":"Segmenting continuous speech is impressive.","rn":[["","What is hard about distinguishing words?","What is hard about distinguishing words?","Speech is continuous sound; No pauses"]]},{"id":7015,"t":"Theories of Language Development","f":"la_spec","s":"On the nature–nurture spectrum, where do the three theories sit?","o":["Behaviourism – nurture; nativism – nature; interactionism – both","Behaviourism – nature; nativism – nurture; interactionism – both","Behaviourism – nurture; nativism – both; interactionism – nature","Behaviourism – both; nativism – nature; interactionism – nurture","Behaviourism – nurture; nativism – nature; interactionism – neither"],"e":"<b>Behaviourism</b> (Skinner, 1957) = <b>nurture</b>; <b>nativism</b> (Chomsky, 1959) = <b>nature</b>; <b>interactionism</b> (Bruner, 1983) = <b>a bit of both</b> (LAD plus LASS).","w":["Reversed.","Nativism is nature.","Behaviourism is nurture.","Interactionism is both."],"k":"Behaviourism nurture; nativism nature; interactionism both.","rn":[["","What are the 3 theories of language development?","What are the 3 theories of language development?","Behaviourism; Nativism; Interactionism"],["","Skinner, Chomsky, Bruner: which theory each?","Skinner, Chomsky, Bruner: which theory each?","Skinner: behaviourism; Chomsky: nativism; Bruner: interactionism"],["","Which theory is nature?","Which theory is nature?","Nativism"]]},{"id":7016,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"Which pairing of theorist, date and work is correct?","o":["Skinner 1957 Verbal Behaviour; Chomsky 1959 review; Bruner 1983 Child’s Talk","Chomsky 1957 Verbal Behaviour; Skinner 1959 review; Bruner 1983 Child’s Talk","Skinner 1957 Verbal Behaviour; Bruner 1959 review; Chomsky 1983 Child’s Talk","Skinner 1959 Verbal Behaviour; Chomsky 1957 review; Bruner 1983 Child’s Talk","Skinner 1957 Child’s Talk; Chomsky 1959 review; Bruner 1983 Verbal Behaviour"],"e":"<b>Skinner (1957)</b>, <i>Verbal Behaviour</i> (behaviourism); <b>Chomsky (1959)</b>, review of Skinner's <i>Verbal Behaviour</i> (nativism, LAD); <b>Bruner (1983)</b>, <i>Child's Talk</i> (interactionism, LASS).","w":["Skinner wrote Verbal Behaviour.","Chomsky wrote the review.","Dates swapped.","Titles swapped."],"k":"Skinner 1957; Chomsky 1959; Bruner 1983.","rn":[["","What was Chomsky's (1959) work?","What was Chomsky's (1959) work?","Review of Skinner's Verbal Behaviour"]]},{"id":7017,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"Who is the leading behaviourist whose 1957 book was Verbal Behaviour?","e":"B.F. Skinner (1957): language is learned like any behaviour through imitation and reinforcement.","k":"Skinner.","a":["skinner","b f skinner","bf skinner","b.f. skinner"],"rn":[["","What was Skinner's (1957) work?","What was Skinner's (1957) work?","Verbal Behaviour"],["","Who is linked to behaviourism (language)?","Who is linked to behaviourism (language)?","B.F. Skinner (1957)"]]},{"id":7018,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"A toddler gets a high five every time he says a phrase, so he keeps saying it. What behaviourist mechanism is this?","e":"Reinforcement (reward) makes a behaviour more likely; punishment makes it less likely.","k":"Reinforcement.","a":["reinforcement","positive reinforcement","reinforce"]},{"id":7019,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"Behaviourism sees the newborn mind as a blank slate written on by experience. Name this philosophical tradition.","e":"Behaviourism fits the empiricist tradition: experience writes on a blank slate.","k":"Empiricism.","a":["empiricism","empiricist","empiricist tradition"],"rn":[["","Which tradition does behaviourism fit?","Which tradition does behaviourism fit?","Empiricism: born a blank slate"]]},{"id":7020,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"The language children hear is limited, full of slips and rarely corrected. What is this argument against behaviourism called?","e":"Poverty of the stimulus: children’s evidence is too limited and messy to explain language learning by experience alone.","k":"Poverty of the stimulus.","a":["poverty of the stimulus","the poverty of the stimulus","poverty of stimulus"],"rn":[["","Poverty of the stimulus","Poverty of the stimulus","Evidence children get is; Too limited and messy"],["","What evidence supports the poverty of the stimulus?","What evidence supports the poverty of the stimulus?","Children rarely corrected; Input is messy"]]},{"id":7021,"t":"Child-Directed Speech","s":"A child says “My foots hurt” and the parent replies “Oh, your feet hurt?” Which problem for behaviourism does this exchange illustrate?","o":["Punishment is mostly missing: adults recast, not correct","Punishment is effective: the child is corrected immediately","Reinforcement is missing: the parent ignores the child","Imitation is perfect: the child copies the parent","Generalisation is absent: the child cannot apply rules"],"e":"Adults <b>rarely say “no, that's wrong”</b>; they usually <b>recast</b> (“your feet hurt?”). So the punishment behaviourism needs is mostly <b>missing</b> – part of the poverty of the stimulus.","w":["Recasting is not punishment.","The parent responds.","“Foots” is not imitation.","The child applied a rule."],"k":"Recasting ≠ punishment → problem for behaviourism.","rn":[["","Example of recasting?","Example of recasting?","Child: \"My foots hurt.\"; Adult: \"Oh, your feet hurt?\""],["","What do adults usually do instead of correcting a child?","What do adults usually do instead of correcting a child?","Recast it correctly; \"Oh, your feet hurt?\""]]},{"id":7022,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"The sentences “Smith believes that the earth is flat”, “Brown believes that Smith believes...” and so on illustrate which problem for behaviourism?","o":["Language has infinite sentences, but evidence is finite","Children over-apply plural rules such as “foots”","Children are rarely corrected for their errors","Children’s input includes false starts, slips and incomplete sentences","Children prefer short sentences to long ones"],"e":"The recursion example shows a language contains an <b>infinite</b> number of sentences while our evidence is <b>finite</b> (Radford et al., 2009). We understand sentences we have never heard, so we cannot simply be copying observed behaviour.","w":["Generalisation problem.","Poverty of the stimulus.","That is the poverty of the stimulus.","Not stated."],"k":"Infinite sentences from finite evidence.","rn":[["","What is the \"infinite sentences\" problem?","What is the \"infinite sentences\" problem?","A language has infinite sentences; Our evidence is only finite"]]},{"id":7023,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"Which set lists the three problems for behaviourism?","o":["Poverty of the stimulus; infinite sentences; generalising beyond imitation","Poverty of the stimulus; too focused on the individual; no LASS","Too much correction; infinite sentences; generalising beyond imitation","Poverty of the stimulus; infinite sentences; no critical period","Recasting; scaffolding; child-directed speech"],"e":"Three problems: (1) <b>poverty of the stimulus</b> (little correction, messy input); (2) <b>infinite sentences from finite evidence</b>; (3) children <b>generalise</b> beyond imitation (“my foots hurt”).","w":["Those are problems for nativism.","Little correction.","Not stated.","Features of CDS."],"k":"Three problems for behaviourism.","rn":[["","What are the 3 problems for behaviourism?","What are the 3 problems for behaviourism?","Poverty of the stimulus; Infinite sentences from finite evidence; Children go beyond imitation"]]},{"id":7024,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"What is the innate device, proposed by Chomsky, that derives a language from limited experience? (full name)","e":"LAD = Language Acquisition Device: children are biologically programmed with it.","k":"LAD.","a":["language acquisition device","lad"],"rn":[["","What does the LAD (Language Acquisition Device) do?","What does the LAD (Language Acquisition Device) do?","Innate device; Derives a language; From limited experience"],["","LAD","LAD","Language Acquisition Device"]]},{"id":7025,"t":"Theories of Language Development","f":"la25","s":"In the model “limited input → LAD → full language”, what is the key point?","o":["The output is far richer than the input; the LAD bridges the gap","The input and output are identical in richness","The input is richer than the output, so learning a language is relatively easy","The LAD replaces the need for any input at all","The output is limited to sentences the child has heard"],"e":"Input: <b>limited and messy</b> experience (poverty of the stimulus). The LAD processes it. Output: a <b>full language</b> able to produce infinitely many sentences. The <b>output is far richer than the input</b>.","w":["Output is richer.","The input is limited and messy.","Input is still needed.","Infinite sentences."],"k":"LAD: rich output from poor input.","rn":[["","LAD model: input and output","LAD model: input and output","Input: experience; Output: language"]]},{"id":7026,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"Which argument comparing dogs and children supports nativism?","o":["A dog learns commands but never judges grammaticality","A dog learns commands but never learns its name","A dog judges grammaticality but cannot speak","A dog and a child learn commands in the same way","A dog learns more slowly because it is rarely corrected"],"e":"<b>Humans vs dogs</b>: a dog can learn simple commands but will never distinguish grammatical from ungrammatical sentences. Something is <b>hard-wired</b> in children that is not in dogs. Also: all languages share a similar underlying grammar.","w":["Not stated.","Dogs cannot judge grammar.","The point is a difference.","Not the point."],"k":"Humans vs dogs supports the LAD.","rn":[["","Why can a dog never learn grammar?","Why can a dog never learn grammar?","Lacks the hard-wiring; Children have (LAD)"]]},{"id":7027,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"Language is very hard to learn if not learned in the pre-school years. What is this limited window called?","e":"The LAD seems to work during a critical period; evidence for nativism.","k":"Critical period.","a":["critical period","the critical period","sensitive period"],"rn":[["","What evidence is there of a critical period?","What evidence is there of a critical period?","Language very hard to learn; If pre-school years missed"]]},{"id":7028,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"What evolutionary explanation is given for the LAD?","o":["People with the capacity were more likely to pass on genes","Parents taught the capacity over many generations","The capacity evolved through reinforcement of speech","The capacity developed with the invention of writing","The capacity evolved from the ability to learn commands"],"e":"The LAD is presumably something we have through <b>evolution</b>: people with this capacity were more likely to <b>pass on their genes</b>.","w":["Not learned.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"LAD evolved: selective advantage.","rn":[["","Why might humans have a LAD?","Why might humans have a LAD?","Evolution; Carriers passed on genes"]]},{"id":7029,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"Who wrote the 1959 review arguing that language is too complex to be learned by reward and punishment alone?","e":"Chomsky’s review of Skinner’s Verbal Behaviour proposed nativism and the LAD.","k":"Chomsky.","a":["chomsky","noam chomsky"],"rn":[["","What was Chomsky's (1959) work?","What was Chomsky's (1959) work?","Review of Skinner's Verbal Behaviour"],["","Who proposed the LAD?","Who proposed the LAD?","Chomsky"]]},{"id":7030,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"What are the problems for nativism identified?","o":["Too focused on the individual; social interaction helps","Too focused on reinforcement and punishment; ignores the innate LAD","Cannot explain the poverty of the stimulus","Overstates the role of parents and teachers","Ignores the critical period for language"],"e":"Problems for nativism: it seems <b>too focused on the individual</b> language learner, and <b>social interaction seems to help</b> language learning. Bruner: “<b>Every LAD needs a LASS</b>.”","w":["That is a criticism of behaviourism.","Nativism explains it.","The opposite.","It relies on it."],"k":"Nativism: too individual; social interaction matters.","rn":[["","What is the main problem for nativism?","What is the main problem for nativism?","Too focused on individual learner; Social interaction seems to help"]]},{"id":7031,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"“Every LAD needs a ...” – complete Bruner’s phrase (abbreviation or full name).","e":"Bruner: every LAD needs a LASS – a Language Acquisition Support System of people around the child.","k":"LASS.","a":["lass","language acquisition support system"],"rn":[["","Complete Bruner's phrase: \"Every LAD needs a...\"","Complete Bruner's phrase: \"Every LAD needs a...\"","LASS"]]},{"id":7032,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"Which statement best describes interactionism?","o":["Biologically prepared for language, but environment matters too","Biologically prepared for language, so environment matters little","Language is learned only through imitation and reward","Only the LASS matters; there is no innate device","Language is learned mainly through school teaching"],"e":"Interactionists agree humans are <b>biologically prepared</b> for language (a LAD) but see the <b>environment</b> as important too. Bruner believed the way language is presented helps children engage, labelling it <b>child-directed speech</b> (Woolfolk et al., 2013).","w":["Environment matters.","Behaviourism.","A LAD is accepted.","Not stated."],"k":"Interactionism: LAD + supportive environment.","rn":[["","What do interactionists believe?","What do interactionists believe?","Biologically prepared for language; Environment also important"]]},{"id":7033,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"Why would talking to a 4-year-old about quantum mechanics fail, according to Bruner's view?","o":["They need support at their level; a LAD is not enough","The child lacks a LAD until about age 5","The child has not been reinforced for complex words","The child has already passed the critical period for language learning","The child cannot distinguish words at age 4"],"e":"Bruner accepted the child might have a LAD but said it is <b>not all you need</b>: you also need <b>support from people around you</b> making things easier to understand. Talking about quantum mechanics is not pitched at her level.","w":["The child has a LAD.","Not reinforcement.","The critical period is pre-school; 4-year-olds are within it.","They can."],"k":"LAD needs LASS: pitch support at the child's level.","rn":[["","Complete Bruner's phrase: \"Every LAD needs a...\"","Complete Bruner's phrase: \"Every LAD needs a...\"","LASS"]]},{"id":7034,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"How does child-directed speech fit into the theories?","o":["It is part of the LASS described by interactionism","It is part of the LAD described by nativism","It is a form of reinforcement in behaviourism","It is evidence for the poverty of the stimulus","It is part of the LAD described by interactionism"],"e":"<b>Child-directed speech is part of the LASS</b> – the environmental support that interactionists (Bruner) see as important alongside the innate LAD.","w":["It is environmental.","Not reinforcement.","It improves the input.","LASS, not LAD."],"k":"Child-directed speech ∈ LASS.","rn":[["","Child-directed speech is part of what?","Child-directed speech is part of what?","The LASS"]]},{"id":7035,"t":"Child-Directed Speech","s":"Which list contains only features of child-directed speech?","o":["Slower rate, higher pitch, varied intonation, stress on key words","Slower rate, lower pitch, varied intonation, stress on key words","Faster rate, higher pitch, varied intonation, stress on key words","Slower rate, higher pitch, monotone, stress on key words","Slower rate, higher pitch, varied intonation, longer sentences"],"e":"Child-directed speech: <b>slower rate</b>, <b>higher pitch</b>, <b>more varied intonation</b>, <b>shorter, simpler sentences</b>, <b>stress on key words</b>, <b>frequent repetition and paraphrase</b>, <b>immediate-environment topics</b>, and <b>recasting/expanding</b> the child's utterances (Lightbown and Spada, 2013).","w":["Higher pitch.","Slower rate.","Varied intonation.","Shorter sentences."],"k":"CDS features.","rn":[["","Child-directed speech: 3 sound features?","Child-directed speech: 3 sound features?","Slower, with pauses; Higher pitch; More varied intonation"]]},{"id":7036,"t":"Child-Directed Speech","s":"Which statement about the topics and vocabulary of child-directed speech is correct?","o":["Child’s interests, here and now, literal; vocabulary suited to stage","Child’s interests, abstract ideas, literal; vocabulary suited to stage","Adult’s interests, here and now, literal; vocabulary suited to stage","Child’s interests, here and now, metaphorical; adult vocabulary","Future events, here and now, literal; vocabulary suited to stage"],"e":"Child-directed speech uses <b>vocabulary tailored to the developmental stage</b>, <b>focuses on the child's interests</b>, emphasises the <b>immediate environment</b>, and is <b>literal and concrete</b> rather than metaphorical and abstract (you wouldn't discuss guilt or justice with a 3-year-old).","w":["Concrete, not abstract.","Child’s interests.","Literal and tailored.","Immediate environment."],"k":"CDS: concrete, here and now, child's interests.","rn":[["","What 3 other qualities does child-directed speech have?","What 3 other qualities does child-directed speech have?","Vocabulary tailored to stage; Focuses on the child's interests; Literal and concrete, not abstract"]]},{"id":7037,"t":"Child-Directed Speech","s":"What are the three purposes of child-directed speech given?","o":["Easier to understand, maintains attention, provides scaffolding","Easier to understand, corrects errors, provides scaffolding","Maintains attention, provides reinforcement, teaches grammar rules","Easier to understand, maintains attention, replaces the LAD","Provides scaffolding, reduces vocabulary, speeds up speech"],"e":"The point of child-directed speech: (1) <b>easier for the child to understand</b>; (2) <b>maintains the child's attention</b>; (3) provides <b>scaffolding</b> – support until they can stand on their own, since language learning is <b>incremental</b>.","w":["It recasts rather than corrects.","Not the purposes.","It supports the LAD.","Not the purposes."],"k":"CDS: understanding, attention, scaffolding.","rn":[["","What are the 3 reasons for child-directed speech?","What are the 3 reasons for child-directed speech?","Easier to understand; Maintains attention; Provides scaffolding"]]},{"id":7038,"t":"Child-Directed Speech","s":"Temporary support given to a learner and withdrawn as they cope alone is called what?","e":"Scaffolding: support until they can stand on their own; child-directed speech provides it.","k":"Scaffolding.","a":["scaffolding","scaffold"],"rn":[["","Scaffolding","Scaffolding","Temporary support; Withdrawn as learner copes alone"]]},{"id":7039,"t":"Child-Directed Speech","s":"Why does varied intonation help language learning?","o":["It keeps attention, giving more chances to practise","It reduces the number of words the child must learn","It replaces the need for repetition and paraphrase","It acts as a reward, reinforcing correct speech","It signals errors without punishing the child"],"e":"Varied intonation creates an environment where children <b>keep paying attention</b> (unlike a monotone lecture), giving them more chances to <b>interact and practise</b>.","w":["Not stated.","Repetition is a separate feature.","Not the reason given.","That is recasting."],"k":"Intonation sustains attention → more practice.","rn":[["","Why use varied intonation?","Why use varied intonation?","Keeps the child's attention"]]},{"id":7040,"t":"Child-Directed Speech","s":"A child says “My foots hurt” and the adult replies “Oh, your feet hurt?” Name this technique.","e":"Recasting: repeating the child’s utterance in a corrected, expanded form rather than correcting them.","k":"Recasting.","a":["recasting","recast","recasting the utterance"],"rn":[["","Recast","Recast","Repeating a child's utterance; Corrected, expanded form"],["","Example of recasting?","Example of recasting?","Child: \"My foots hurt.\"; Adult: \"Oh, your feet hurt?\""],["","What do adults usually do instead of correcting a child?","What do adults usually do instead of correcting a child?","Recast it correctly; \"Oh, your feet hurt?\""]]},{"id":7041,"t":"Doctor-Child Communication","s":"Which tips belong to “create a positive communication environment”?","o":["Serve and return, non-verbal communication, first names","Ask questions, monitor responses, ask them to repeat back","Vocabulary, sentence length and time to respond","One question at a time and pause between instructions","Serve and return, monitor responses, time to respond"],"e":"<b>Create a positive environment</b>: <b>serve and return</b> (turn-taking), <b>non-verbal communication</b> (body language, facial expression, getting down to the child's level) and <b>use of first names</b>.","w":["Gauge their level.","Tailor your language.","More top tips.","Mixed groups."],"k":"Positive environment: serve and return, non-verbal, first names.","rn":[["","How do you create a positive communication environment?","How do you create a positive communication environment?","\"Serve and return\"; Non-verbal communication; Use first names"]]},{"id":7042,"t":"Doctor-Child Communication","s":"Name the turn-taking technique in which the child offers something and the adult acknowledges and responds back, like tennis.","e":"Serve and return creates a positive communication environment.","k":"Serve and return.","a":["serve and return","serve-and-return"],"rn":[["","Serve and return","Serve and return","Turn-taking, like tennis"],["","How do you create a positive communication environment?","How do you create a positive communication environment?","\"Serve and return\"; Non-verbal communication; Use first names"],["","How do you do serve and return?","How do you do serve and return?","Acknowledge what child offers; Respond"]]},{"id":7043,"t":"Doctor-Child Communication","s":"Which set of strategies helps a doctor gauge a child's language level?","o":["Ask questions, monitor responses, ask them to repeat back","Use first names, non-verbal cues and serve and return","Think about vocabulary, sentence length and response time","Rely on the child’s age and the milestone table","Ask only yes/no questions and watch body language"],"e":"<b>Gauge the level</b>: <b>ask questions</b>, <b>monitor responses</b>, <b>ask them to repeat the information back</b>. Age gives a first guess, but you should investigate.","w":["Create environment.","Tailor language.","Age is a first guess.","Not stated."],"k":"Gauge: ask, monitor, repeat back.","rn":[["","How do you gauge a child's language level?","How do you gauge a child's language level?","Ask questions; Monitor responses; Ask them to repeat back"]]},{"id":7044,"t":"Doctor-Child Communication","s":"Asking a child to repeat information back links to which other clinical concept?","o":["Assessing capacity to consent","Recasting: repeating with corrections","Serve and return: turn-taking","Scaffolding: temporary support","Reinforcement: rewarding correct answers with praise"],"e":"Repeating back is the same tool used to assess <b>capacity to consent</b>: a person must be able to <b>understand, retain</b> and <b>communicate</b> their decision. It checks understanding.","w":["Different concept.","Different concept.","Related, but the clinical link is capacity.","Not the clinical link."],"k":"Repeat-back ↔ capacity assessment.","rn":[["","Repeating information back is also used to assess what?","Repeating information back is also used to assess what?","Mental capacity to consent"]]},{"id":7045,"t":"Doctor-Child Communication","s":"Which three things should a doctor think about to tailor their language?","o":["Vocabulary, sentence length/complexity, time to respond","Serve and return, non-verbal cues, first names","Ask questions, monitor responses, repeat back","Pitch, volume and accent of the doctor’s voice","Vocabulary, pitch of voice and the number of questions asked at once"],"e":"<b>Tailor your language</b>: think about <b>vocabulary</b>, <b>sentence length and complexity</b>, and the <b>time you're giving them to respond</b>.","w":["Create environment.","Gauge level.","Not stated.","Pitch and number of questions are not the three."],"k":"Tailor: vocabulary, sentence length, response time.","rn":[["","How do you tailor your language to a child?","How do you tailor your language to a child?","Vocabulary; Sentence length and complexity; Time given to respond"]]},{"id":7046,"t":"Doctor-Child Communication","s":"A doctor fires five quick questions at a 4-year-old because the appointment is short. What should they do instead?","o":["One question at a time, pausing to let them respond","Ask the parent instead, as children cannot answer quickly","Ask fewer but longer, multi-part questions","Use more complex language to save time","Ask yes/no questions only, as quickly as possible"],"e":"There is a bias towards firing lots of questions quickly; resist it. <b>One question at a time</b> and <b>pausing</b> is more productive, giving time to <b>process and respond</b>.","w":["Children can answer.","One at a time.","Keep it simple.","Pause."],"k":"One question at a time; give time.","rn":[["","Why ask a child one question at a time?","Why ask a child one question at a time?","Short visits bias firing questions; Gets the best from child"]]},{"id":7047,"t":"Doctor-Child Communication","s":"Which wording best follows the tip “keep it literal and concrete” when explaining a cannula to a young child?","o":["“A little scratch”","“We will perform a cannulation”","“This intervention optimises fluid therapy”","“It's like a metaphorical bridge”","“Venous access is required”"],"e":"Keep it <b>literal and concrete</b>, avoiding metaphors and abstract terms: “<b>a scratch</b>” not “a cannulation”.","w":["Technical.","Abstract.","Metaphorical.","Technical."],"k":"Literal and concrete wording.","rn":[["","Example of literal and concrete language?","Example of literal and concrete language?","\"A scratch\" not \"a cannulation\""]]},{"id":7048,"t":"Doctor-Child Communication","s":"Which of the following is listed under “more top tips”?","o":["Encourage questions","Use first names","Monitor their responses","Think about vocabulary","Serve and return"],"e":"More top tips: <b>encourage them to ask questions</b>, <b>keep their attention</b>, <b>pause between instructions</b>, <b>ask one question at a time</b>, <b>keep it literal and concrete</b>.","w":["Create environment.","Gauge level.","Tailor language.","Create environment."],"k":"More tips list.","rn":[["","Which 2 extra tips keep a child engaged?","Which 2 extra tips keep a child engaged?","Encourage questions; Keep their attention"]]},{"id":7049,"t":"Doctor-Child Communication","s":"How can a doctor keep a young child's attention?","o":["Varying intonation and things to look at or play with","Speaking in a calm monotone and asking many questions","Using longer sentences and technical vocabulary","Avoiding eye contact to reduce pressure","Using varied intonation and talking to the parent"],"e":"Ways to keep attention: <b>intonation</b> (voice going up and down) and having <b>things to look at or play with</b>.","w":["Monotone loses attention.","Short and simple.","Not stated.","Talk to the child."],"k":"Attention: intonation + objects.","rn":[["","How can you keep a child's attention?","How can you keep a child's attention?","Intonation; Things to look at; Things to play with"]]},{"id":7050,"t":"Doctor-Child Communication","s":"Why should doctor–child communication tips be treated as “generic”?","o":["They are rules of thumb that must be adapted to each child","They work for every child in exactly the same way, so need no adapting","They apply mainly to adults, not children","They replace the need for person-centred care","They apply only to children with autism"],"e":"The tips are <b>generic</b>: a rule of thumb, not a method that works for everyone. People learn at different rates and in different ways – e.g. <b>autistic children</b> may need different approaches. Use <b>person-centred care</b> to apply them to the individual.","w":["Children differ.","For children.","The opposite.","Generic for all children."],"k":"Generic tips; individualise.","rn":[["","Do the communication tips work for every child?","Do the communication tips work for every child?","No: generic rules of thumb; e.g. autistic children differ"]]},{"id":7051,"t":"Doctor-Child Communication","s":"Which pairing links a theory idea to a doctor's action correctly?","o":["Scaffolding – support, then check by asking them to repeat back","Scaffolding – give support, then remove it immediately","Poverty of the stimulus – correct every error explicitly to improve the input","LAD – rely on age alone and skip checking","Behaviourism – avoid praising the child’s answers"],"e":"The guide's link table: <b>scaffolding</b> → give support, then check by asking them to <b>repeat back</b>; development rates → gauge; milestones → point to where it hurts; CDS → tailor; literal/concrete → avoid metaphors; LASS → positive environment.","w":["Gradually removed.","Recast instead.","Gauge level.","Not stated."],"k":"Link theory to practice.","rn":[["","How do you gauge a child's language level?","How do you gauge a child's language level?","Ask questions; Monitor responses; Ask them to repeat back"],["","Repeating information back is also used to assess what?","Repeating information back is also used to assess what?","Mental capacity to consent"],["","Scaffolding","Scaffolding","Temporary support; Withdrawn as learner copes alone"]]},{"id":7052,"t":"Doctor-Child Communication","s":"Which practice reflects the interactionist idea that social support matters?","o":["A positive environment: serve and return, names","Relying on the child’s innate LAD only","Using adult vocabulary to stretch the child","Asking the parent instead of the child","Correcting each error so that the child learns faster"],"e":"<b>LASS / interactionism</b>: social support matters → create a <b>positive environment</b> (serve and return, first names, non-verbal cues).","w":["Nativism.","Tailor language.","Talk to the child.","Recast instead."],"k":"LASS → positive environment.","rn":[["","How do you create a positive communication environment?","How do you create a positive communication environment?","\"Serve and return\"; Non-verbal communication; Use first names"]]},{"id":7053,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"Why is it worth slowing down in a time-pressured appointment with a child?","o":["Pausing lets the child process and show understanding","Pausing makes appointments shorter overall","Pausing reinforces correct answers","Pausing lets the parent answer for the child","Pausing reduces the total number of questions you will need to ask"],"e":"Appointments are time-pressured, but it is <b>more productive</b> to slow down and pause, giving the child time to <b>process and respond</b>, so you can tell whether they've understood.","w":["Not stated.","Not reinforcement.","Talk to the child.","Not the reason."],"k":"Slowing down is more productive.","rn":[["","Child-directed speech: 3 sound features?","Child-directed speech: 3 sound features?","Slower, with pauses; Higher pitch; More varied intonation"],["","What 2 understanding skills does a child have by age 3?","What 2 understanding skills does a child have by age 3?","Identifies body parts; Answers \"where?\" questions"],["","Give an example of pragmatics in a child's language.","Give an example of pragmatics in a child's language.","\"Can we play?\"; \"Daddy's tired.\"; Child understands: no, not now"]]},{"id":7054,"t":"Doctor-Child Communication","s":"A 3-year-old with earache sits on her mother’s lap. Which opening is most appropriate for the doctor?","o":["Crouch to her level: “Hi Mia, I’m Sam. Can you show me where it hurts?”","Stand at the desk: “Can you describe the character of the pain?”","“Mia, does it hurt, how long, and is it worse at night?”","Talk only to the mother, as 3-year-olds cannot describe pain","“We’ll do an otoscopic examination of the tympanic membrane.”"],"e":"By age 3 children use short sentences, identify body parts and answer “where?” questions. A positive environment (getting to their level, first names) plus one concrete question at a time suits this stage.","w":["Describing the “character” of pain is too abstract for age 3.","Asking several questions at once overloads a young child; ask one at a time.","By 3 a child can identify body parts and answer “where?” questions.","Technical terms break the rule “keep it literal and concrete”."],"k":"Age 3: ask “where?” and let them point.","rn":[["","How do you gauge a child's language level?","How do you gauge a child's language level?","Ask questions; Monitor responses; Ask them to repeat back"]]},{"id":7055,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"A parent says their 2-year-old uses about 20 words and two-word phrases, while a neighbour's 2-year-old says much more. What is the best response?","o":["Typical for 2; rates differ, so gauge this child","Below average; the child is likely to lack a LAD","Below average; a 2-year-old should already be using about 450 words","Typical for 1; the child is a year behind","Below average; the child needs correcting more"],"e":"By 2 a child typically has <b>~20 words</b> and <b>two-word sentences</b>. People <b>develop at different rates</b>, so comparisons should be cautious; gauge the individual child.","w":["Typical for 2.","~450 is age 3.","Typical for 2.","Recast, not correct."],"k":"Use milestones flexibly.","rn":[["","Why not judge a child's language level from age alone?","Why not judge a child's language level from age alone?","Different rates of development; Age is a first guess; Then check"]]},{"id":7056,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"Which observation is the strongest evidence against pure behaviourism?","o":["Children say forms like “foots” that no adult says","Children imitate the words their parents use","Children respond to praise such as high fives","Children learn their own names by age 1","Children are rarely corrected by their parents for mistakes"],"e":"“Foots” cannot be <b>imitated</b>, because no adult says it; the child has <b>extracted and over-applied a rule</b>. This shows children <b>generalise beyond imitation</b>, a key problem for behaviourism.","w":["Fits behaviourism.","Fits behaviourism.","Fits behaviourism.","Also a problem, but “foots” directly defeats imitation."],"k":"Over-generalisation refutes pure imitation.","rn":[["","How do children go beyond imitation?","How do children go beyond imitation?","They generalise from examples; e.g. \"My foots hurt\""]]},{"id":7057,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"Which statement correctly contrasts Skinner and Bruner?","o":["Skinner: reinforcement (nurture). Bruner: LAD + LASS (both)","Skinner: LAD (nature). Bruner: reinforcement (nurture)","Skinner: reinforcement (nurture). Bruner: LAD only (nature)","Skinner: LASS (both). Bruner: reinforcement (nurture)","Skinner: blank slate (nature). Bruner: LAD + LASS (both)"],"e":"<b>Skinner</b>: behaviourism (nurture) – imitation and reinforcement. <b>Bruner</b>: interactionism – the LAD needs a <b>LASS</b> (nature + nurture).","w":["Swapped.","Bruner added the LASS.","Skinner is behaviourism.","Blank slate is nurture."],"k":"Skinner vs Bruner.","rn":[["","LAD vs LASS","LAD vs LASS","LAD: child's brain (nature); LASS: people around (nurture)"],["","Complete Bruner's phrase: \"Every LAD needs a...\"","Complete Bruner's phrase: \"Every LAD needs a...\"","LASS"],["","Skinner, Chomsky, Bruner: which theory each?","Skinner, Chomsky, Bruner: which theory each?","Skinner: behaviourism; Chomsky: nativism; Bruner: interactionism"]]},{"id":7058,"t":"Doctor-Child Communication","s":"An autistic child is not responding to the doctor's usual child-friendly approach. What is the best next step?","o":["Tips are generic; adapt using person-centred care","Tips are universal; repeat them more slowly","Talk only to the parent from now on","Switch to more abstract explanations","Ask several questions at once to engage them"],"e":"The lecturer specifically noted that <b>autistic children</b> may need different ways of being communicated with; the tips are generic, so use <b>person-centred care</b> to apply them individually.","w":["Adapt them.","Still engage the child.","Literal.","One at a time."],"k":"Individualise for autistic children.","rn":[["","Do the communication tips work for every child?","Do the communication tips work for every child?","No: generic rules of thumb; e.g. autistic children differ"]]},{"id":7059,"t":"Child-Directed Speech","s":"Which feature of child-directed speech directly addresses the “poverty of the stimulus” by improving the evidence children receive?","o":["Recasting utterances into correct forms","Rewarding correct utterances with praise","Using a slower rate of speech","Focusing on the child’s own interests","Asking the child to repeat back"],"e":"Child-directed speech improves the input: recasting gives the child a <b>correct version</b> without punishment, and repetition and paraphrase give more clear examples – part of the <b>LASS</b> that supports the LAD.","w":["That is reinforcement.","Helps understanding, not correct forms.","Helps attention, not correct evidence.","Gauging."],"k":"CDS enriches input (recasting, repetition).","rn":[["","Recast","Recast","Repeating a child's utterance; Corrected, expanded form"]]},{"id":7060,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"Which statement about language in dogs and humans is most consistent with nativism?","o":["Dogs learn commands, but only humans can judge grammaticality","Dogs and humans both judge grammaticality with training","Dogs learn commands faster than children learn words","Dogs and children both learn language by reinforcement","Dogs lack a LASS, so they cannot learn commands"],"e":"A dog can learn <b>simple commands</b> (conditioning) but will never tell grammatical from ungrammatical sentences – something is <b>hard-wired</b> in children: support for nativism.","w":["Dogs cannot.","Not stated.","Language is more than commands.","Not the point."],"k":"Dog example → innate capacity."},{"id":7061,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"A 4-year-old in clinic can tell a story about how they fell over. Which approach to history-taking is most appropriate?","o":["Begin open questions, as 4-year-olds tell stories","Use only gestures, as 4-year-olds cannot explain","Use complex terminology, as they know ~1,000 words","Ask only “where?” questions, as for a 1-year-old","Ask only the parent, as stories are unreliable"],"e":"By 4 children <b>tell stories</b> and use <b>5-word sentences</b>, so the lecturer suggested you can <b>start asking open questions</b> – while still tailoring vocabulary and checking understanding.","w":["They can speak.","Keep tailoring vocabulary.","“Where?” is age 3.","Ask the child."],"k":"Age 4: open questions possible.","rn":[["","At what age can you start asking open questions?","At what age can you start asking open questions?","By 4 (stories, longer sentences)"]]},{"id":7062,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"Which age-appropriate expectation is <b>incorrect</b>?","o":["A 1-year-old can describe their symptoms in sentences","A 2-year-old can make it known they are in pain","A 3-year-old can point to where it hurts","A 4-year-old can tell a story about a fall","A 5-year-old is likely to ask “why?”"],"e":"A 1-year-old has only a <b>few words</b> and follows <b>simple instructions</b>; they cannot describe a history in sentences. The other expectations match the milestones.","w":["Correct.","Correct.","Correct.","Correct."],"k":"Match clinical expectations to milestones."},{"id":7063,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"A 2-year-old says “Tummy ow” repeatedly. Which milestone does this best show?","o":["Combining words to make wants known","Following simple instructions","Answering “where?” questions","Telling a short story about the pain","Using the past tense"],"e":"By 2 children use <b>two-word sentences</b> and <b>make desires known</b>; the lecturer noted a 2-year-old can make it known they are in pain and want it to stop.","w":["Age 1.","Age 3.","That is age 4.","Age 5."],"k":"Age 2: make desires known.","rn":[["","What can a child do with language by age 2?","What can a child do with language by age 2?","Two-word sentences, including names; Makes desires known"]]},{"id":7064,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"A child knows their street name and recites a nursery rhyme with mistakes that no one corrects. What does this suggest about both development and theory?","o":["Typical of 4; lack of correction = poverty of the stimulus","Typical of 4; lack of correction = behaviourist punishment","Typical of 2; lack of correction = poverty of the stimulus","Typical of 5; lack of correction disproves nativism","Typical of 3; lack of correction = scaffolding"],"e":"Knowing street name and nursery rhymes is typical <b>by 4</b>. In the video the niece made mistakes and <b>nobody corrected her</b>, yet she will still end up speaking correctly – an example of the <b>poverty of the stimulus</b>.","w":["No punishment.","Age 4.","It supports nativism.","Age 4."],"k":"Uncorrected errors = poverty of the stimulus.","rn":[["","What evidence supports the poverty of the stimulus?","What evidence supports the poverty of the stimulus?","Children rarely corrected; Input is messy"],["","Poverty of the stimulus","Poverty of the stimulus","Evidence children get is; Too limited and messy"],["","What are the 3 problems for behaviourism?","What are the 3 problems for behaviourism?","Poverty of the stimulus; Infinite sentences from finite evidence; Children go beyond imitation"]]},{"id":7065,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"About how many words does a typical 3-year-old use?","e":"~20 (2), ~450 (3), ~1,000 (4), ~1,500 (5): an explosion of words between 2 and 3.","k":"~450.","a":["450","about 450","around 450"],"rn":[["","How many words by age 3?","How many words by age 3?","~450"]]},{"id":7066,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"Which statement best explains why interactionism is described as a compromise?","o":["It accepts a LAD but adds social support (LASS)","It rejects both a LAD and social support","It accepts reinforcement but rejects the LAD","It accepts the LAD and rejects social support","It accepts a LASS but rejects the LAD"],"e":"Interactionism = a bit of <b>nativism</b> (humans have a LAD; dogs do not) and a bit of <b>behaviourism</b> (experience and environment matter): “every LAD needs a LASS”.","w":["It accepts both.","It accepts a LAD.","That is nativism.","It accepts a LAD."],"k":"Interactionism = LAD + LASS.","rn":[["","What is the LASS (Language Acquisition Support System)?","What is the LASS (Language Acquisition Support System)?","The social support; Around a child"]]},{"id":7067,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"Which piece of evidence would a nativist use, and which would an interactionist add?","o":["Nativist: poor evidence; interactionist: social input also helps","Nativist: reinforcement; interactionist: punishment","Nativist: social input helps; interactionist: poor evidence","Nativist: dogs learn grammar; interactionist: children cannot","Nativist: no critical period; interactionist: a critical period"],"e":"Nativists point to the <b>poverty of the stimulus</b> and the LAD; interactionists accept this but add that <b>social interaction seems to help</b> (child-directed speech, LASS).","w":["Behaviourist ideas.","Swapped.","Dogs cannot.","Nativists cite a critical period."],"k":"Nativist and interactionist evidence.","rn":[["","What is the main problem for nativism?","What is the main problem for nativism?","Too focused on individual learner; Social interaction seems to help"],["","What evidence supports the poverty of the stimulus?","What evidence supports the poverty of the stimulus?","Children rarely corrected; Input is messy"]]},{"id":7068,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"Children are rarely explicitly corrected yet learn correct grammar. Which theory of language development does this most challenge?","e":"Behaviourism relies on reinforcement and punishment; the lack of correction (poverty of the stimulus) challenges it.","k":"Behaviourism.","a":["behaviourism","behaviorism","behaviourist","skinner"],"rn":[["","What are the 3 theories of language development?","What are the 3 theories of language development?","Behaviourism; Nativism; Interactionism"]]},{"id":7069,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"What is meant by Bruner's phrase “Every LAD needs a LASS”?","o":["An innate capacity needs a supportive social environment","An innate capacity needs explicit grammar teaching","A social environment works without any innate capacity","Every child needs a teacher to correct their errors","Every child needs reinforcement to activate the LAD"],"e":"The <b>LAD</b> (child's brain, nature) needs the <b>LASS</b> (people around the child, nurture): the innate device is <b>not all you need</b>.","w":["Not teaching.","LAD is accepted.","Not correction.","Not reinforcement."],"k":"LAD needs LASS."},{"id":7070,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"Which statement about the evidence children hear is used against behaviourism?","o":["False starts, incomplete sentences and slips","Grammatically perfect sentences spoken slowly by adults","Explicit grammar lessons from parents","Mostly single words with clear pauses","The same sentences repeated for every child"],"e":"“The language children are exposed to includes <b>false starts, incomplete sentences, and slips of the tongue</b>, and yet they learn to distinguish between grammatical and ungrammatical sentences” (Lightbown and Spada, 2013).","w":["The input is messy.","Not given.","Continuous speech.","Not stated."],"k":"Messy input argument.","rn":[["","How is language input messy?","How is language input messy?","False starts; Incomplete sentences; Slips of the tongue"],["","What flaws does language input contain?","What flaws does language input contain?","False starts; Incomplete sentences; Slips of the tongue"]]},{"id":7071,"t":"Child-Directed Speech","s":"A nurse talks to a toddler slowly, in a higher voice, with varied intonation and repetition. What is this style of speech called?","e":"Child-directed speech: slower rate, higher pitch, varied intonation, short simple sentences, stress on key words, repetition, recasting.","k":"Child-directed speech.","a":["child-directed speech","child directed speech","motherese","infant directed speech","parentese"],"rn":[["","Child-directed speech: 3 sound features?","Child-directed speech: 3 sound features?","Slower, with pauses; Higher pitch; More varied intonation"],["","Child-directed speech: 3 sentence features?","Child-directed speech: 3 sentence features?","Shorter, simpler sentences; Stress on key words; Repetition and paraphrase"]]},{"id":7072,"t":"Child-Directed Speech","s":"Which adult response best demonstrates recasting?","o":["“Me goed park.” – “Oh, you went to the park?”","“Me goed park.” – “No, say ‘I went to the park’.”","“Me goed park.” – “Good talking!” (high five)","“Me goed park.” – “Can you say that again?”","“Me goed park.” – “Me goed park.”"],"e":"Recasting repeats the child's utterance in a <b>correct, expanded</b> form without saying it is wrong.","w":["That is correction.","Reinforcement.","Not recasting.","Exact repetition is not a recast."],"k":"Recast = correct model, no criticism."},{"id":7073,"t":"Child-Directed Speech","s":"Which feature of child-directed speech is most closely related to “pause between instructions” in doctor communication?","o":["Slower rate of delivery","Higher pitch","Stress on key words","Topics in the immediate environment","Recasting"],"e":"Child-directed speech has a <b>slower rate</b> with pauses between words; the doctor's tip to <b>pause between instructions</b> and give time to respond applies the same idea.","w":["Different feature.","Different feature.","Different feature.","Different feature."],"k":"Slower rate ↔ pausing."},{"id":7074,"t":"Child-Directed Speech","s":"Why do the milestones matter for child-directed speech?","o":["They show what vocabulary level to pitch at","They show the exact age each word should appear","They show which children lack a LAD","They show when to stop using child-directed speech","They show when children should be corrected"],"e":"Tailoring vocabulary is why it helps to know the <b>milestones</b>: they tell you what level to pitch at, remembering that <b>children develop at different rates</b>.","w":["Rates differ.","Not stated.","Scaffolding is gradual.","Recast instead."],"k":"Milestones guide pitch."},{"id":7075,"t":"Child-Directed Speech","s":"Which statement is <b>not</b> a feature of child-directed speech?","o":["Metaphorical and abstract language","Frequent repetition and paraphrase","Topics emphasising the immediate environment","Shorter, simpler sentence patterns","More varied intonation"],"e":"Child-directed speech is <b>literal and concrete</b>, not metaphorical and abstract.","w":["A feature.","A feature.","A feature.","A feature."],"k":"CDS is literal/concrete."},{"id":7076,"t":"Doctor-Child Communication","s":"A doctor crouches to a child's eye level, smiles and introduces herself by first name. Which group of tips is she using?","o":["Creating a positive communication environment","Gauging the child’s level of understanding","Tailoring vocabulary and sentence length","Pausing between instructions","Serve and return"],"e":"<b>Non-verbal communication</b> (getting down to the child's level, facial expression) and <b>use of first names</b> are tips for <b>creating a positive communication environment</b>.","w":["Different group.","Different group.","More tips.","Part of the same group, but the actions are non-verbal cues and names."],"k":"Non-verbal + names = positive environment.","rn":[["","How do you create a positive communication environment?","How do you create a positive communication environment?","\"Serve and return\"; Non-verbal communication; Use first names"],["","What are the first 2 groups of doctor-child communication tips?","What are the first 2 groups of doctor-child communication tips?","Create a positive environment; Gauge their language level"]]},{"id":7077,"t":"Doctor-Child Communication","s":"After explaining how to use an inhaler to a 5-year-old, a doctor asks “Can you show me and tell me what we do first?” Which tip is this?","o":["Gauging understanding by asking them to repeat back","Serve and return to keep the conversation going","Tailoring language to their vocabulary","Encouraging the child to ask questions","Using stress on key words"],"e":"Asking the child to <b>repeat the information back</b> is a way to <b>gauge</b> understanding (linked to assessing capacity).","w":["Different tip.","Different tip.","Different tip.","Different tip."],"k":"Repeat-back checks understanding.","rn":[["","Repeating information back is also used to assess what?","Repeating information back is also used to assess what?","Mental capacity to consent"],["","How do you gauge a child's language level?","How do you gauge a child's language level?","Ask questions; Monitor responses; Ask them to repeat back"]]},{"id":7078,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"Which change would most improve this instruction to a 3-year-old: “If you experience any discomfort post-procedure, please notify a member of staff”?","o":["“If it hurts, tell me.”","Add more technical detail","Say it faster","Ask three questions after it","Use a monotone"],"e":"Tailor vocabulary and sentence length; keep it <b>literal and concrete</b>, short and simple – “If it hurts, tell me.”","w":["Opposite.","Opposite.","One question at a time.","Varied intonation helps."],"k":"Short, simple, concrete wording.","rn":[["","What can a child do with language by age 4?","What can a child do with language by age 4?","Tells stories; Uses 5-word sentences"],["","At what age can a child point to where it hurts?","At what age can a child point to where it hurts?","By 3"]]},{"id":7079,"t":"Doctor-Child Communication","s":"A doctor tells a 4-year-old: “The nurse will put a tiny tube in your hand, then you’ll have some medicine, then you can go to the playroom,” all in one breath. The child looks confused. What is the single best change?","o":["Pause between instructions and check understanding after each","Repeat the whole sentence faster","Use more technical terms so it sounds official","Give the information only to the parent","Add more detail about how the medicine works"],"e":"Top tips: pause between instructions, ask one question at a time, keep it literal and concrete, and gauge understanding by asking them to repeat back. Child-directed speech uses a slower rate and shorter, simpler sentences.","w":["Speed makes it harder to process.","Technical terms make it less literal and concrete.","The child should still be included and checked.","More detail adds load."],"k":"Pause between instructions; check understanding.","rn":[["","Which 3 extra tips make instructions easier?","Which 3 extra tips make instructions easier?","Pause between instructions; One question at a time; Keep it literal, concrete"]]},{"id":7080,"t":"Doctor-Child Communication","s":"A 5-year-old keeps asking “why?” about his asthma inhaler. Which response is best?","o":["Welcome the questions, answer simply and concretely, then ask him to show you","Tell him he will understand when he is older","Answer each “why?” with full pharmacological detail","Ignore the questions to finish on time","Ask the parent to answer instead"],"e":"At 5 children ask questions (the “why?” stage). Doctors should encourage questions, keep explanations literal and concrete, and check understanding by asking the child to repeat back or show what to do.","w":["Encouraging questions is a top tip.","Explanations should be literal, concrete and pitched at his level.","One question at a time, with time to respond, is better than rushing.","The child should be included."],"k":"Encourage questions; check by demonstration.","rn":[["","How do you gauge a child's language level?","How do you gauge a child's language level?","Ask questions; Monitor responses; Ask them to repeat back"]]},{"id":7081,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"A 3-year-old says “Mouses ran.” What can you infer?","o":["They apply grammar rules creatively, beyond imitation","They are imitating a sibling who says “mouses”","They have learned “mouses” as a single new word from a sibling","They are behind for age and need correction","They are copying a nursery rhyme"],"e":"Like “foots”, “mouses” shows the child has extracted rules and <b>over-applied</b> them – <b>generalising beyond imitation</b>, a problem for behaviourism.","w":["No one says it.","No one says it; it is rule-based.","Recast, not correct.","Not stated."],"k":"Over-regularisation = rule use.","rn":[["","What does \"My foots hurt\" show?","What does \"My foots hurt\" show?","Extracted the plural -s rule; Over-applied it; Not imitation"]]},{"id":7082,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"Which statement about what children have achieved by age 5 is correct?","o":["Distinguish and say words, meanings, grammar, pragmatics","Distinguish and say words, but not yet grammar","Distinguish and say words, meanings, but not pragmatics","Pronounce words, but cannot yet split speech into words","Only bilingual children have learned grammar by 5"],"e":"By 5 children have learned to <b>distinguish</b> and <b>pronounce</b> words, understand <b>meaning</b>, learn <b>grammar</b> and <b>pragmatics</b>; some learn more than one language – “pretty impressive”.","w":["They learn grammar.","They learn pragmatics.","They can.","All children learn grammar."],"k":"By 5: words, meaning, grammar, pragmatics.","rn":[["","What 3 language levels have children learned by age 5?","What 3 language levels have children learned by age 5?","Meaning (semantics); Grammar; Pragmatics"]]},{"id":7083,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"Which statement best explains why development being “orderly” matters for doctors?","o":["Simple skills precede complex ones, so milestones guide","All children reach each milestone at the same age","Children can skip earlier stages if taught well","Development is unpredictable, so milestones tend to mislead doctors","Order applies only after age 5"],"e":"<b>Development is relatively orderly</b>: simple before complex, in roughly the same order. Milestones are useful guides, though rates differ.","w":["Rates differ.","Not stated.","It is relatively orderly.","Not stated."],"k":"Orderly sequence → milestones useful."},{"id":7084,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"Which statement about Skinner's behaviourism is <b>false</b>?","o":["It proposes an innate language acquisition device","It treats language like any other behaviour","It relies on imitation and reinforcement","It was a big movement in the first half of the 20th century","It fits the empiricist blank-slate idea"],"e":"The <b>LAD</b> is <b>Chomsky's nativist</b> idea, not behaviourist.","w":["True.","True.","True.","True."],"k":"LAD ≠ behaviourism.","rn":[["","What does the LAD (Language Acquisition Device) do?","What does the LAD (Language Acquisition Device) do?","Innate device; Derives a language; From limited experience"],["","LAD","LAD","Language Acquisition Device"]]},{"id":7085,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"A family moves abroad and their 8-year-old struggles to learn a first language for the first time after limited early exposure. Which concept best explains this?","o":["A critical period during which the LAD seems to work","The poverty of the stimulus in the new country","A lack of reinforcement from new teachers","Too little child-directed speech at age 8","A missing LASS in the new environment"],"e":"There is evidence of a <b>limited window</b>: if language is not learned in the <b>pre-school years</b> it is really difficult – the LAD works during a <b>critical period</b>.","w":["Not the best fit.","Not stated.","Not the best fit.","The key issue is timing."],"k":"Critical period for language.","rn":[["","What evidence is there of a critical period?","What evidence is there of a critical period?","Language very hard to learn; If pre-school years missed"]]},{"id":7086,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"Which theorist proposed that the way language is presented to children (child-directed speech) helps them engage with it?","e":"Jerome Bruner (1983, Child’s Talk): interactionism – LAD plus LASS.","k":"Bruner.","a":["bruner","jerome bruner"],"rn":[["","Bruner: how does presenting language to children help?","Bruner: how does presenting language to children help?","They engage more easily"],["","Who is linked to interactionism (language)?","Who is linked to interactionism (language)?","Jerome Bruner (1983)"],["","Who named child-directed speech?","Who named child-directed speech?","Jerome Bruner (1983)"]]},{"id":7087,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"A child has heard only a limited, error-filled set of sentences, yet produces and understands sentences she has never heard. Which theorist’s explanation relies most on an innate structure to bridge this gap?","o":["Chomsky – a Language Acquisition Device","Skinner – reinforcement of imitated speech","Bruner – a Language Acquisition Support System only","Woolfolk – general principles of development","Skinner – the blank slate written on by experience"],"e":"The poverty of the stimulus and the infinite-sentences argument led Chomsky (1959) to propose an innate LAD that derives a full language from limited experience.","w":["Reinforcement cannot explain sentences never heard or reinforced.","Bruner added social support to the LAD; the innate part is Chomsky’s.","These describe development, not the mechanism.","A blank slate is the empiricist view the gap argues against."],"k":"Limited input, rich output → Chomsky’s LAD.","rn":[["","What does the LAD (Language Acquisition Device) do?","What does the LAD (Language Acquisition Device) do?","Innate device; Derives a language; From limited experience"],["","LAD","LAD","Language Acquisition Device"]]},{"id":7088,"t":"Child-Directed Speech","s":"Which pair of actions reflects child-directed speech principles applied by a doctor?","o":["Using simple words and pausing to let the child respond","Using adult vocabulary and speaking quickly","Correcting the child's grammar explicitly and asking many questions","Talking about abstract ideas and avoiding repetition","Speaking in a monotone and avoiding eye contact"],"e":"Tailoring vocabulary and pausing apply CDS principles (simpler sentences, slower rate).","w":["Opposite.","Opposite.","Opposite.","Opposite."],"k":"Apply CDS clinically.","rn":[["","Child-directed speech: 3 sound features?","Child-directed speech: 3 sound features?","Slower, with pauses; Higher pitch; More varied intonation"],["","What can a child do with language by age 1?","What can a child do with language by age 1?","Recognises own name; Says a few words; Follows simple instructions"],["","What is hard about distinguishing words?","What is hard about distinguishing words?","Speech is continuous sound; No pauses"]]},{"id":7089,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"Why might a child ask “why?” after every answer at age 5?","o":["They realise they can always ask “why?” again","They have not understood the earlier answers","They are imitating a parent’s habit of asking","They are testing whether answers are true","They cannot yet use the past tense"],"e":"Around school-starting age, the <b>“why?” stage</b>: children realise that whenever you answer, they can still ask “why?”. Doctors should expect and encourage questions.","w":["Not stated.","Not the reason.","Not stated.","They can by 5."],"k":"Age 5 “why?” stage."},{"id":7090,"t":"Doctor-Child Communication","s":"Why is it good practice to encourage a young child to ask questions in clinic?","o":["It fits the “why?” stage and serve and return","It shortens the appointment","It replaces the need to gauge their level","It reinforces correct grammar","It reduces the need for scaffolding"],"e":"Children ask questions by age 5; encouraging questions keeps them engaged (serve and return) and helps check understanding.","w":["Not stated.","Still gauge.","Not the reason.","Not the reason."],"k":"Encourage questions.","rn":[["","How do you create a positive communication environment?","How do you create a positive communication environment?","\"Serve and return\"; Non-verbal communication; Use first names"],["","How do you do serve and return?","How do you do serve and return?","Acknowledge what child offers; Respond"],["","Serve and return","Serve and return","Turn-taking, like tennis"]]},{"id":7091,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"A doctor uses the child's first name, offers a toy, and responds enthusiastically whenever the child points at things. Which theoretical idea underpins this?","o":["Interactionism: social support (LASS)","Nativism’s emphasis on the innate LAD","Behaviourism’s emphasis on reinforcement","The poverty of the stimulus","The critical period for language"],"e":"Creating a <b>positive environment</b> (serve and return, names, non-verbal cues) reflects the <b>LASS / interactionist</b> idea that social support matters.","w":["Not social.","Not the focus.","Not relevant.","Not relevant."],"k":"Positive environment ↔ LASS.","rn":[["","What is the LASS (Language Acquisition Support System)?","What is the LASS (Language Acquisition Support System)?","The social support; Around a child"]]},{"id":7092,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"Which statement about the infinite-sentences argument is correct?","o":["We understand unheard sentences, so it is not copying","We understand only sentences we have heard before","Children hear every sentence they will ever need","It supports behaviourism, as children imitate","It concerns pronunciation rather than grammar"],"e":"Each of us can understand recursive sentences at any level of complexity though nobody has heard most of them – so we cannot just be copying observed behaviour.","w":["The opposite.","Evidence is finite.","It is against behaviourism.","Grammar."],"k":"Novel sentences → not imitation."},{"id":7093,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"What do recursive sentences (“Brown believes that Smith believes that…”) suggest about language learning?","o":["Languages seem too complex to learn from experience","Languages are simple enough to learn by imitation and reward","Recursion is learned through reinforcement","Recursion is used only by adults","Recursion shows children are blank slates"],"e":"“Languages seem <b>too complex to be learned by experience</b>, especially given the <b>limitations of the evidence</b>.”","w":["The opposite.","Not reinforcement.","Not stated.","Against blank slate."],"k":"Complexity + limited evidence.","rn":[["","What can the limited stimulus not explain?","What can the limited stimulus not explain?","Language learning by experience alone"],["","What is the main problem for nativism?","What is the main problem for nativism?","Too focused on individual learner; Social interaction seems to help"]]},{"id":7094,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"A child identifies body parts, answers “where?” questions and uses short sentences with some plurals. What age (in years) is most typical?","e":"By 3: ~450 words, short sentences, identifies body parts, answers “where?”, some plurals.","k":"About 3.","a":["3","three","3 years","age 3","three years"],"rn":[["","What 2 understanding skills does a child have by age 3?","What 2 understanding skills does a child have by age 3?","Identifies body parts; Answers \"where?\" questions"]]},{"id":7095,"t":"Doctor-Child Communication","s":"Which statement about first names in doctor–child communication is correct?","o":["Use the child’s name and give your own","Use the child’s surname to stay professional","Avoid names so the child feels less pressure","Use only the parent’s name when speaking","Introduce yourself by title rather than by name"],"e":"<b>Use of first names</b> – the child's name and introducing yourself by name – is part of creating a positive communication environment.","w":["First names.","Names help.","Talk to the child.","First names."],"k":"First names help.","rn":[["","What can a child do with language by age 1?","What can a child do with language by age 1?","Recognises own name; Says a few words; Follows simple instructions"],["","How do you create a positive communication environment?","How do you create a positive communication environment?","\"Serve and return\"; Non-verbal communication; Use first names"]]},{"id":7096,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"Which pair matches skill and example correctly?","o":["Grammar – learning plural endings","Pragmatics – producing sounds correctly","Pronunciation – reading between the lines","Semantics – splitting speech into words","Distinguishing words – understanding implied meaning"],"e":"<b>Grammar</b>: rules for combining words, e.g. <b>plural endings</b>. Pragmatics: meaning beyond the literal; pronunciation: producing sounds; semantics: meaning; distinguishing: segmenting speech.","w":["Pronunciation.","Pragmatics.","Distinguishing words.","Pragmatics."],"k":"Match language skills.","rn":[["","What 3 language levels have children learned by age 5?","What 3 language levels have children learned by age 5?","Meaning (semantics); Grammar; Pragmatics"]]},{"id":7097,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"A researcher shows that children whose carers use more child-directed speech, turn-taking and recasting learn vocabulary faster, though all children have the innate capacity. Which theory does this best support?","o":["Interactionism","Nativism alone","Behaviourism alone","The empiricist blank-slate view","None – it disproves an innate capacity"],"e":"Interactionism (Bruner): humans are biologically prepared (LAD), but the environment matters too (LASS), including child-directed speech – “every LAD needs a LASS”.","w":["Nativism alone underplays social input.","Behaviourism denies an innate capacity.","That is behaviourism’s view.","The finding assumes the innate capacity exists."],"k":"Innate + social support = interactionism.","rn":[["","What are the 3 theories of language development?","What are the 3 theories of language development?","Behaviourism; Nativism; Interactionism"],["","Which theory is a bit of both?","Which theory is a bit of both?","Interactionism"]]},{"id":7098,"t":"Child-Directed Speech","s":"Which of the following would most likely <b>fail</b> to keep a 3-year-old's attention in clinic?","o":["Speaking in a monotone with long explanations","Varying intonation as you speak","Offering something to look at or play with","Using their first name","Using short, simple sentences"],"e":"A monotone with long explanations is the opposite of child-directed speech; varied intonation and objects help keep attention.","w":["Helps.","Helps.","Helps.","Helps."],"k":"Avoid monotone and long talk."},{"id":7099,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"Which statement about the relationship between LAD and dogs and the critical period is consistent with nativism?","o":["A hard-wired capacity dogs lack, best in an early window","A hard-wired capacity dogs share, best in an early window","A learned capacity dogs lack, available at any age","A hard-wired capacity dogs lack, available at any age","A learned capacity from parents, best in an early window"],"e":"Nativism: a hard-wired capacity (absent in dogs) working during a <b>critical period</b> in the pre-school years.","w":["Dogs lack it.","Hard-wired.","Critical period.","Innate."],"k":"LAD: human-specific, critical period.","rn":[["","Why can a dog never learn grammar?","Why can a dog never learn grammar?","Lacks the hard-wiring; Children have (LAD)"]]},{"id":7100,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"A 2-year-old's mother says “Wave bye-bye” and the child waves. A 1-year-old does the same. Which statement is correct?","o":["Following simple instructions is expected by 1, so both are typical","Following simple instructions is expected by 2, so only the older one is typical","Following simple instructions is expected by 3, so neither is typical","Following simple instructions is expected by 5","Following instructions shows reinforcement, not development"],"e":"By <b>1</b> children follow <b>simple instructions</b> (e.g. “Michael, wave bye-bye”), so both are typical.","w":["By 1.","By 1.","By 1.","Not the point."],"k":"Simple instructions by age 1.","rn":[["","What simple instruction might a 1-year-old follow?","What simple instruction might a 1-year-old follow?","\"Michael, wave bye-bye.\""]]},{"id":7101,"t":"Child-Directed Speech","s":"Which statement best describes “stress on key words” in child-directed speech?","o":["Emphasising the important word","Speaking every word with equal emphasis","Saying the key word quietly to calm the child","Replacing the key word with a simpler one","Repeating the key word in a corrected form"],"e":"<b>Stress on key words</b>: emphasise the important word.","w":["Opposite.","Not stated.","That is tailoring vocabulary.","That is recasting."],"k":"Stress key words."},{"id":7102,"t":"Child-Directed Speech","s":"Which of the following is an example of scaffolding being withdrawn appropriately?","o":["Simpler language with a toddler, more complex as they grow","The same simple language with a teenager","Complex language from the start to stretch the child","Stopping all simplification once they reach 3","Correcting errors more often as they grow"],"e":"Scaffolding is <b>gradually removed</b> as the child can cope alone; language learning is incremental.","w":["Gradual removal.","Start simple.","Gradual.","Recast."],"k":"Gradually remove scaffolding."},{"id":7103,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"Two cousins: one says first words late but catches up quickly; the other speaks early. Both reach the same skills by school. Which principle of development does this show?","o":["People develop at different rates","Development is relatively orderly","Development takes place gradually","Development stops at age 5","Development depends only on reinforcement"],"e":"Three principles: people develop at different rates; development is relatively orderly; it takes place gradually. Different speeds to the same end point = different rates.","w":["Orderliness is about the sequence (simple → complex).","Gradual means step by step, not differing speeds.","Not stated.","That is a behaviourist claim, not a principle."],"k":"Different rates.","rn":[["","What are the 3 general principles of development?","What are the 3 general principles of development?","People develop at different rates; Development is relatively orderly; Development takes place gradually"]]},{"id":7104,"t":"Doctor-Child Communication","s":"Which consultation behaviour best follows the advice about time pressure?","o":["Slowing down despite time pressure","Asking several questions together to save time","Talking only to the parent to save time","Giving all instructions at once","Skipping checks of understanding"],"e":"Despite time pressure, it is more productive to <b>slow down</b>, <b>pause</b> and ask <b>one question at a time</b>.","w":["One at a time.","Talk to the child.","Pause between instructions.","Check understanding."],"k":"Slow down despite time pressure."},{"id":7105,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"Which statement is the best overall summary of the practical message?","o":["Use insights from all three theories at the child’s level","Use behaviourism, as it best explains language","Use nativism, as language is innate","Speak to children as you would to adults","Rely on the milestone table alone to judge the child’s level"],"e":"There are “probably some insights in each” theory; the goal is to use them – with milestones and child-directed speech – to communicate with children at their level (C-G-T + More).","w":["Insights in each.","Insights in each.","Tailor language.","Gauge too."],"k":"Integrate theory into communication.","rn":[["","Child-directed speech","Child-directed speech","Simplified, melodic speech; Adults use with young children"],["","What are the first 2 groups of doctor-child communication tips?","What are the first 2 groups of doctor-child communication tips?","Create a positive environment; Gauge their language level"],["","What non-verbal communication helps with children?","What non-verbal communication helps with children?","Body language; Facial expression; Getting to child's level"]]},{"id":7106,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"Which of these is an example of making desires known rather than following instructions?","o":["A child says “More juice”","A child waves when asked","A child turns when called","A child stops when told “no”","A child points when asked “where?”"],"e":"Making desires known (by 2): combining words to get things to happen – “More juice”. Following instructions and responding to names are age-1 skills; answering “where?” is age 3.","w":["Following instructions.","Recognising name.","Following instructions.","Answering “where?”."],"k":"Making desires known vs following instructions.","rn":[["","What can a child do with language by age 1?","What can a child do with language by age 1?","Recognises own name; Says a few words; Follows simple instructions"]]},{"id":7107,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"A child says “I goed home.” His father replies, “You went home? Lovely.” Which two ideas does this exchange illustrate?","o":["Over-applied grammar rule; recasting rather than punishment","Imitation of the father; punishment of the error","Pragmatics; explicit grammar teaching","Over-applied grammar rule; reinforcement of the error","Distinguishing words; scaffolding withdrawn"],"e":"“Goed” (like “foots”) shows a rule (past tense -ed) over-applied – not imitation. Adults rarely correct explicitly; they recast into a correct form, part of the poverty of the stimulus for behaviourism.","w":["No adult says “goed”, and the father does not punish.","The father does not teach rules explicitly.","Recasting models the correct form; it does not reward the error.","Not shown."],"k":"Over-regularisation + recast.","rn":[["","What does \"My foots hurt\" show?","What does \"My foots hurt\" show?","Extracted the plural -s rule; Over-applied it; Not imitation"]]},{"id":7108,"t":"Child-Directed Speech","s":"Which statement about child-directed speech and the immediate environment is correct?","o":["Topics focus on the here and now","Topics focus on future events","Topics focus on abstract ideas","Topics focus on the adult’s interests","Topics avoid the child’s surroundings"],"e":"CDS topics emphasise the child's <b>immediate environment</b> – what is here and now – and focus on the child's interests.","w":["Here and now.","Concrete.","Child’s interests.","Immediate environment."],"k":"Here and now."},{"id":7109,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"Which statement about the poverty of the stimulus and nativism is correct?","o":["A key argument for nativism: limited evidence implies a LAD","A key argument for behaviourism: limited evidence is enough","A key argument against the LAD","A key argument for interactionism only","A key argument that children get too much input"],"e":"If the evidence is too poor to explain language, something innate (the <b>LAD</b>) must fill the gap – a central nativist argument.","w":["Against behaviourism.","For the LAD.","Primarily nativism.","Too little."],"k":"Poverty of stimulus → LAD.","rn":[["","Poverty of the stimulus","Poverty of the stimulus","Evidence children get is; Too limited and messy"],["","Does nativism say babies are born speaking?","Does nativism say babies are born speaking?","No; Built-in structure; Learns from limited evidence"]]},{"id":7110,"t":"Doctor-Child Communication","s":"Which communication habit is most likely to confuse a 3-year-old?","o":["Metaphors such as “an army in your blood”","Using the child’s first name","Asking them to point to where it hurts","Pausing between instructions","Repeating key words"],"e":"Keep it <b>literal and concrete</b>; metaphors and abstract ideas are not suitable for young children.","w":["Helps.","Helps.","Helps.","Helps."],"k":"Avoid metaphors with young children."},{"id":7111,"t":"Doctor-Child Communication","s":"Which of the following best illustrates “non-verbal communication” as a doctor tip?","o":["Smiling and getting down to their level","Writing a clear referral letter","Using a calm, steady monotone","Asking questions quickly to keep pace","Using the correct technical terms"],"e":"Non-verbal communication: <b>body language</b>, <b>facial expression</b>, <b>getting down to the child's level</b>.","w":["Not communication with the child.","Monotone loses attention.","One at a time.","Keep it literal."],"k":"Non-verbal tips.","rn":[["","What non-verbal communication helps with children?","What non-verbal communication helps with children?","Body language; Facial expression; Getting to child's level"]]},{"id":7112,"t":"Doctor-Child Communication","s":"Which statement about the effect of serve and return is correct?","o":["Children keep practising and want to join in","Children practise less as adults take over","It replaces child-directed speech","It works only with adults","It is a form of punishment"],"e":"Serve and return creates a positive environment in which children <b>keep practising</b> and <b>want to be part of the conversation</b>.","w":["The opposite.","Not stated.","For children.","Not punishment."],"k":"Serve and return encourages practice."},{"id":7113,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"Which of these explains why age is only a “first guess” for a child's language level?","o":["People develop at different rates","Development is always identical","Milestones are unreliable for every child","Language never develops before 5","Age is not recorded in clinic"],"e":"Because people <b>develop at different rates</b>, age gives a <b>first guess</b>, then you investigate by asking questions and monitoring responses.","w":["Opposite.","Too strong.","Opposite.","Not related."],"k":"Age = first guess.","rn":[["","What are the 3 general principles of development?","What are the 3 general principles of development?","People develop at different rates; Development is relatively orderly; Development takes place gradually"],["","Why not judge a child's language level from age alone?","Why not judge a child's language level from age alone?","Different rates of development; Age is a first guess; Then check"]]},{"id":7114,"t":"Child-Directed Speech","s":"An adult reading to a toddler slows down, raises their pitch, varies their intonation, stresses key words and repeats them. Which three purposes does this speech serve?","o":["Easier to understand, keeps attention, provides scaffolding","Corrects errors, punishes mistakes, keeps attention","Replaces the LAD, speeds learning, corrects grammar","Shortens interactions, reinforces habits, adds vocabulary","Keeps attention only"],"e":"Child-directed speech is easier for the child to understand, maintains attention and provides scaffolding (support until they can stand on their own).","w":["Child-directed speech recasts rather than corrects or punishes.","It supports the LAD; it does not replace it.","It is about understanding, attention and scaffolding.","Two other purposes are missing."],"k":"Understand, attention, scaffold.","rn":[["","What are the 3 reasons for child-directed speech?","What are the 3 reasons for child-directed speech?","Easier to understand; Maintains attention; Provides scaffolding"]]},{"id":7115,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"Which statement about the role of punishment in language learning is supported?","o":["Explicit punishment is rare, weakening behaviourism","Children are regularly punished for errors","Punishment is the main way grammar is learned","Recasting is a form of punishment","Punishment explains forms like “foots”"],"e":"Children rarely have their speech explicitly corrected; adults recast instead. So punishment is <b>mostly missing</b>, weakening behaviourism.","w":["Rare.","Rare.","Not punishment.","Not imitation or punishment."],"k":"Little punishment in language learning.","rn":[["","Behaviourism: last 2 steps of language learning?","Behaviourism: last 2 steps of language learning?","Reinforced or punished; Form habits"]]},{"id":7116,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"Which statement about B.F. Skinner is correct?","o":["Leading behaviourist, known for pigeon studies","The leading nativist, known for the LAD","The leading interactionist, known for the LASS","The author of Child’s Talk (1983)","The author of the 1959 review of Verbal Behaviour"],"e":"Skinner was behaviourism's leading proponent, famous for experiments with animals such as <b>pigeons</b>; his <i>Verbal Behaviour</i> (1957) was reviewed by Chomsky (1959).","w":["Chomsky.","Bruner.","Bruner.","Chomsky."],"k":"Skinner: behaviourism, pigeons."},{"id":7117,"t":"Doctor-Child Communication","s":"A 5-year-old asks why they need an injection. What is the best response approach?","o":["Encourage it; answer simply; check understanding","Give a detailed explanation of how vaccines work","Ask the parent to answer instead","Tell them to wait until after the injection","Avoid answering so they don’t get upset"],"e":"Expect and <b>encourage questions</b> (the “why?” stage), answer in <b>literal, concrete</b> language and check understanding.","w":["Literal and simple.","Engage the child.","Encourage questions.","Encourage questions."],"k":"Encourage and answer questions simply.","rn":[["","What 2 understanding skills does a child have by age 3?","What 2 understanding skills does a child have by age 3?","Identifies body parts; Answers \"where?\" questions"]]},{"id":7118,"t":"Child-Directed Speech","s":"Which of the following is the best description of “paraphrase” in child-directed speech?","o":["Saying the same thing another way","Saying the same thing more slowly","Saying the same thing more quietly","Saying the child’s words in correct form","Saying the key word with emphasis"],"e":"<b>Frequent repetition and paraphrase</b>: say it again, or say it another way.","w":["Rate.","Not stated.","Recasting.","Stress on key words."],"k":"Paraphrase = say it another way."},{"id":7119,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"Which of these is most consistent with child-directed speech being part of the LASS?","o":["Adults adjust their speech to support the child","Children are born with a full language","Adults speak to children as they do to adults","Children learn mainly from television","Children learn mainly through punishment"],"e":"Child-directed speech is adults adjusting speech to <b>support</b> the child – part of the <b>Language Acquisition Support System</b>.","w":["Not LASS.","Not adjusting.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"CDS = adult support (LASS).","rn":[["","Child-directed speech is part of what?","Child-directed speech is part of what?","The LASS"],["","What is the LASS (Language Acquisition Support System)?","What is the LASS (Language Acquisition Support System)?","The social support; Around a child"],["","Child-directed speech","Child-directed speech","Simplified, melodic speech; Adults use with young children"]]},{"id":7120,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"A student summarises: “Behaviourism explains everything because children imitate.” Which counter-example is most decisive?","o":["New forms like “foots”, and understanding unheard sentences","Children imitate their parents’ words and phrases very closely","Children respond to praise such as high fives","Children learn their own names by age 1","Children enjoy repeating nursery rhymes"],"e":"Novel forms like “foots” and understanding unheard recursive sentences show children <b>go beyond imitation</b> and <b>finite evidence</b> – two problems for behaviourism.","w":["Fits behaviourism.","Fits behaviourism.","Fits behaviourism.","Fits behaviourism."],"k":"Decisive counter-evidence to behaviourism.","rn":[["","What 2 speech skills does a child have by age 3?","What 2 speech skills does a child have by age 3?","Short sentences; Forms some plurals"]]},{"id":7121,"t":"Language Development in Pre-School Years","s":"A 3-year-old boy is brought to the GP with a sore leg after a fall at nursery. He is shy but talks in short sentences, and when he arrives he says \"My foots hurt.\" The GP wants to find out exactly where the pain is. She remembers what children of his age can usually do with language.<br><br>Which approach is best suited to his likely language level?","o":["Ask \"Where does it hurt?\" and let him point","Ask him to tell a long story about how the fall happened","Ask him to describe the pain using abstract words such as \"dull\" or \"radiating\"","Give him a list of three instructions at once and see if he follows them","Ask only yes/no questions, because 3-year-olds cannot identify body parts or answer \"where?\""],"e":"Step 1: by age 3 children have about 450 words, use short sentences, identify body parts, answer \"where?\" questions and form some plurals (as in \"My foots hurt\"). Step 2: so the GP can ask \"where does it hurt?\" and let him point, while keeping language literal and concrete rather than abstract.","w":["Telling stories is a skill typically seen by age 4.","Language should be literal and concrete, not abstract.","The tips say pause between instructions and ask one question at a time.","By age 3 children can identify body parts and answer \"where?\" questions."],"k":"Age 3: body parts + \"where?\" questions → ask where it hurts.","rn":[["","At what age can a child point to where it hurts?","At what age can a child point to where it hurts?","By 3"]]},{"id":7122,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"A parent records her 3-year-old daughter for a week. The child says \"I goed to the park\" and \"two mouses\", although no adult around her uses these forms. The parent rarely corrects her, and the speech she hears at home is full of false starts and unfinished sentences. Yet the child is learning to tell grammatical from ungrammatical sentences.<br><br>Which conclusion about theories of language development do these observations support?","o":["They challenge behaviourism, because children generalise rules and the input is poor","They support behaviourism, because the child is imitating adult speech closely","They show that reinforcement and punishment fully explain grammar learning","They support behaviourism, because her errors are being punished and corrected","They show language is learned only through child-directed speech, and that biology plays no part in it at all"],"e":"Step 1: forms like \"goed\" and \"mouses\" are not heard from adults, so the child is generalising a rule beyond imitation, and the input is limited and messy with little correction (the poverty of the stimulus). Step 2: these are problems for behaviourism (Skinner), which says language is learned through imitation and reinforcement, and they fit nativism's idea of a LAD (Language Acquisition Device) that derives language from limited experience.","w":["No adult uses these forms, so she is not imitating.","Correction is rare, so reinforcement cannot fully explain it.","Her errors are rarely corrected.","Interactionism still accepts biological readiness for language."],"k":"Over-generalisation + poverty of the stimulus → problems for behaviourism.","rn":[["","What evidence supports the poverty of the stimulus?","What evidence supports the poverty of the stimulus?","Children rarely corrected; Input is messy"],["","What are the 3 problems for behaviourism?","What are the 3 problems for behaviourism?","Poverty of the stimulus; Infinite sentences from finite evidence; Children go beyond imitation"]]},{"id":7123,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"A student writes an essay arguing that children are born with a device that lets them build a language from limited input, so the way adults speak to them makes no difference. Her tutor agrees that humans are biologically prepared for language, but points to evidence that social interaction helps language learning and quotes \"every LAD needs a LASS\".<br><br>Which theory does the tutor hold, and what does LASS refer to?","o":["Interactionism (Bruner); the Language Acquisition Support System, e.g. child-directed speech","Nativism (Chomsky); the Language Acquisition Device","Behaviourism (Skinner); reinforcement and punishment","Nativism (Bruner); an innate grammar","Behaviourism (Chomsky); imitation of adult speech habits, rewarded until they become automatic"],"e":"Step 1: the student's view is nativism (Chomsky): language is innate via a LAD, but this is criticised for being too focused on the individual learner. Step 2: the tutor holds interactionism (Bruner, 1983), which accepts biological readiness but stresses the environment; the LASS (Language Acquisition Support System) is the social support around the child, such as child-directed speech.","w":["That is the student's nativist view, and LAD is the device, not LASS.","Behaviourism denies the innate readiness the tutor accepts.","Bruner is an interactionist, not a nativist.","Chomsky is a nativist, not a behaviourist."],"k":"Bruner: every LAD needs a LASS (social support).","rn":[["","What is the LASS (Language Acquisition Support System)?","What is the LASS (Language Acquisition Support System)?","The social support; Around a child"],["","LASS","LASS","Language Acquisition Support System"]]},{"id":7124,"t":"Child-Directed Speech","s":"A mother is talking to her 18-month-old son at bath time. She speaks slowly in a high voice that goes up and down, uses very short sentences, stresses the key word, and keeps repeating herself about the toy duck in the bath. When he says \"Duck swim\", she replies \"Yes, the duck is swimming!\"<br><br>What is she using, and what are its purposes?","o":["Child-directed speech; easier to understand, holds attention and gives scaffolding","Behaviourist punishment; to stop the child making grammar errors","Child-directed speech; to give the child abstract and metaphorical vocabulary","Pragmatics; to teach the child to split speech into separate words","Serve and return; to test the child's language level by asking him to repeat it back to her"],"e":"Step 1: slower rate, higher pitch, varied intonation, short simple sentences, stress on key words, repetition, here-and-now topics and recasting (\"Yes, the duck is swimming!\") are features of child-directed speech. Step 2: its point is that it is easier for the child to understand, it maintains attention, and it provides scaffolding (support until they can stand on their own).","w":["She is not punishing; she is recasting.","Child-directed speech is literal and concrete, not abstract.","Pragmatics is how we do things with words, not what she is using.","She is not asking him to repeat back information."],"k":"Child-directed speech: simpler, melodic, recasting → understanding, attention, scaffolding.","rn":[["","What are the 3 reasons for child-directed speech?","What are the 3 reasons for child-directed speech?","Easier to understand; Maintains attention; Provides scaffolding"]]},{"id":7125,"t":"Child-Directed Speech","s":"A 3-year-old girl in the emergency department tells the nurse, \"My tummy hurted yesterday.\" The nurse wants to respond in a way that supports the child's language learning, as adults naturally do in child-directed speech. She avoids telling the child she is wrong.<br><br>Which response best illustrates the feature the nurse is using?","o":["\"Oh, your tummy hurt yesterday?\"","\"No, that's wrong, say 'hurt' not 'hurted'.\"","\"Please describe the quality of the abdominal discomfort in more detail for me.\"","\"Hurted is not a word.\"","Saying nothing and moving on to the next question"],"e":"Step 1: in child-directed speech, adults repeat the child's utterance but expand or recast it into a grammatically correct sentence. Step 2: \"Oh, your tummy hurt yesterday?\" repeats her meaning in a correct form without explicit correction, just as \"My foots hurt\" → \"Oh, your feet hurt?\" in the lecture.","w":["Explicit correction is not the recasting feature, and children are rarely corrected anyway.","Abstract, complex language does not suit a 3-year-old.","This corrects her without recasting.","Saying nothing loses the chance to recast and acknowledge her."],"k":"Recast: repeat the child's words in a correct, expanded form.","rn":[["","Example of recasting?","Example of recasting?","Child: \"My foots hurt.\"; Adult: \"Oh, your feet hurt?\""]]},{"id":7126,"t":"Doctor-Child Communication","s":"A 5-year-old boy who is small for his age attends clinic, and the doctor first assumes he has the language of a much younger child. The boy then asks \"Why?\" after each answer and describes the colours of his inhaler. The doctor remembers the general principles of development.<br><br>Which principle explains the doctor's error, and what should the doctor do?","o":["People develop at different rates; gauge his level by questions, responses and repeat-back","Development is always identical by age; trust age alone","Development is disorderly; ignore milestones completely","Development happens suddenly; wait until he is older before talking to him","People develop at the same rate; use adult medical vocabulary with every child of school age"],"e":"Step 1: the general principles of development are that people develop at different rates, development is relatively orderly and it takes place gradually, so a child's language level should not be assumed from age (or size) alone. Step 2: the doctor should gauge the level by asking questions, monitoring responses and asking the child to repeat information back; this boy's \"why?\" questions and naming of colours fit a typical 5-year-old.","w":["People develop at different rates.","Development is relatively orderly, so milestones are a useful starting guess.","Development takes place gradually, and he can be spoken to now.","People develop at different rates, and language should be tailored, not adult."],"k":"Different rates → gauge each child's level; don't rely on age.","rn":[["","How do you gauge a child's language level?","How do you gauge a child's language level?","Ask questions; Monitor responses; Ask them to repeat back"],["","What are the 3 general principles of development?","What are the 3 general principles of development?","People develop at different rates; Development is relatively orderly; Development takes place gradually"]]},{"id":7127,"t":"Doctor-Child Communication","s":"A junior doctor tells a 4-year-old girl: \"We're going to do a cannulation, so stay still, put your arm out, look away and take a deep breath, then you'll feel a sharp scratch which is like a bee sting.\" The girl looks confused and starts to cry. A senior colleague suggests changes based on doctor-child communication tips.<br><br>Which change best fits the lecture's tips?","o":["Use literal, concrete words and give one instruction at a time with pauses","Use more medical terms so she understands exactly what will happen","Speak faster so the procedure is over sooner","Give all the instructions again in one long sentence","Avoid eye contact and stand above her so she knows the doctor is in charge of what happens"],"e":"Step 1: the doctor used abstract vocabulary (\"cannulation\"), a metaphor and many instructions at once. Step 2: the tips are to tailor vocabulary and sentence length, keep it literal and concrete (e.g. \"a scratch\" not \"a cannulation\"), pause between instructions and ask one thing at a time, while creating a positive environment with non-verbal cues at the child's level.","w":["Medical terms are abstract and should be avoided.","Children need time to respond, so speaking faster is unhelpful.","Shorter sentences and pauses are advised.","Getting down to the child's level is part of positive non-verbal communication."],"k":"Literal, concrete; one instruction at a time; pause.","rn":[["","Which 3 extra tips make instructions easier?","Which 3 extra tips make instructions easier?","Pause between instructions; One question at a time; Keep it literal, concrete"]]},{"id":7128,"t":"Doctor-Child Communication","s":"A paediatric doctor greets a nervous 4-year-old by kneeling down to his eye level, smiling and saying \"Hi Sam, I'm Dr Ana.\" When Sam holds up his toy dinosaur, she says \"Wow, a dinosaur! What's his name?\" and waits for him to answer before moving on.<br><br>Which tips for a positive communication environment is she using?","o":["Non-verbal communication, first names and serve and return","Gauging his level by asking for repeat-back","Tailoring sentence length and complexity only","Behaviourist reinforcement of correct grammar","Child-directed recasting of grammar errors"],"e":"Step 1: creating a positive communication environment involves \"serve and return\" (turn-taking like tennis), non-verbal communication (body language, facial expression, getting down to the child's level) and use of first names. Step 2: kneeling and smiling are non-verbal, using names on both sides is first names, and responding to the dinosaur he \"served\" and waiting is serve and return.","w":["She is not asking him to repeat anything back.","She is doing more than tailoring sentence length.","She is not reinforcing grammar.","He has made no grammar error to recast."],"k":"Positive environment: serve and return, non-verbal, first names.","rn":[["","How do you create a positive communication environment?","How do you create a positive communication environment?","\"Serve and return\"; Non-verbal communication; Use first names"]]},{"id":7129,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"A health visitor checks three children. Child A uses about 20 words and says \"Mummy juice\" to ask for a drink. Child B tells stories in 5-word sentences and knows her street name and nursery rhymes. Child C recognises his own name and follows simple instructions such as \"wave bye-bye\".<br><br>Which ages best match these children?","o":["A: 2 years; B: 4 years; C: 1 year","A: 1 year; B: 3 years; C: 2 years","A: 3 years; B: 5 years; C: 1 year","A: 2 years; B: 5 years, as she uses the past tense and asks \"why?\"; C: 2 years","A: 4 years; B: 2 years; C: 3 years"],"e":"Step 1: by 1 a child recognises their name, says a few words and follows simple instructions (child C); by 2 they have about 20 words and use two-word sentences to make desires known (child A). Step 2: by 4 they have about 1,000 words, tell stories, use 5-word sentences and know their last name, street name and nursery rhymes (child B).","w":["Child A's 20 words and two-word sentences fit age 2.","Child A fits age 2 and child B fits age 4.","Child B does not use the past tense or ask \"why?\"; child C fits age 1.","The ages are in the wrong order."],"k":"1: name/instructions; 2: ~20 words, two-word; 4: stories, 5-word sentences.","rn":[["","What evidence is there of a critical period?","What evidence is there of a critical period?","Language very hard to learn; If pre-school years missed"]]},{"id":7130,"t":"Theories of Language Development","s":"A linguistics student argues with a behaviourist friend. The friend says children simply copy sentences they have heard and are rewarded for them. The student points out that every day her 5-year-old says new sentences that nobody has ever said to him, and that any language contains an infinite number of possible sentences.<br><br>Which problem for behaviourism is the student raising, and which theory does it support?","o":["Infinite sentences from finite evidence; nativism's LAD","Poverty of the stimulus is not a problem; behaviourism is supported","Infinite sentences from finite evidence; behaviourism, because more rewards are given","Children never generalise; interactionism","Pragmatics; behaviourism, because children learn what people mean from rewards for using the words"],"e":"Step 1: a language contains an infinite number of sentences, but our evidence is necessarily finite (Radford et al.), so children cannot have copied every sentence they produce. Step 2: this is a problem for behaviourism and supports nativism (Chomsky), which proposes a LAD that derives a language from limited experience.","w":["Poverty of the stimulus is itself a problem for behaviourism.","This argument undermines, not supports, behaviourism.","Children do generalise, as \"My foots hurt\" shows.","The student's point is about infinite sentences, not pragmatics."],"k":"Infinite sentences from finite evidence → supports a LAD.","rn":[["","What is the \"infinite sentences\" problem?","What is the \"infinite sentences\" problem?","A language has infinite sentences; Our evidence is only finite"],["","What are the 3 problems for behaviourism?","What are the 3 problems for behaviourism?","Poverty of the stimulus; Infinite sentences from finite evidence; Children go beyond imitation"]]}]);
